From: noname <noname@example.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 22:40:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 27/27] read wl_display_flush as the byte count it is

flushDisplay() treated a zero return from wl_display_flush as the only
success, but libwayland returns the number of bytes it sent: zero only
when nothing was queued, a positive count when the commit went out, and
-1 with errno on failure. Every present commits and flushes bytes, so
the success arm was never taken: m_flushFailures climbed at the present
rate (the logs read flushFailed=897 at committed=901), the deferred-fd
list was never forgotten on a real flush, and the first warning reported
whatever errno an unrelated call had left behind ("flush failed: File
exists (display error 0)"). Test the sign instead, so a successful flush
forgets the descriptors libwayland already closed and the 240-entry
tripwire measures a compositor that really stopped reading.

Greppable markers: wayland-video fps, flush failed
---
--- a/opennow-qt/src/streaming/rendering/WaylandVideoSurface.cpp
+++ b/opennow-qt/src/streaming/rendering/WaylandVideoSurface.cpp
@@ -957,7 +957,14 @@
 void WaylandVideoSurface::flushDisplay()
 {
     if (!m_display) return;
-    if (wl_display_flush(m_display) == 0) {
+    // wl_display_flush answers with the number of bytes it sent, so a
+    // successful flush is any value from zero up. Only -1 is a failure, and
+    // only -1 with EAGAIN means requests are still queued. Testing for zero
+    // read every present as a failure: the surface commits and flushes bytes
+    // on every frame, so the counter ran at the present rate and errno was
+    // whatever the last unrelated call had left there ("flush failed: File
+    // exists").
+    if (wl_display_flush(m_display) >= 0) {
         // Everything queued so far reached the compositor, so libwayland has
         // already closed the descriptors those requests carried.
         forgetDeferredFds();

